{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/776820-let-s-talk-about-art","id":776820,"title":"Let's talk about ART.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-11-29T14:51:01-08:00","id":776820,"title":"Let's talk about ART.","content":"Hello everyone!<br /> <br /> Just like the headline says, I want to open a discussion about artwork and artistic directions in RPG books nowadays (particularly cWoD). The reason is I recently read a post on enWorld, which in a developer os a 3rd party campaign setting for Pathfinder and now DnD 5e mentioned that if they want to do the book in physical form for 5E it would mean the re-doing of the whole book because the DnD costumers maybe wouldn't tolerate the black and white artwork of the books. That's springed a thought in my mind, because I noticed that basicaly since V20, the main art direction of both WoDs is also full colour artwork. Note, that I don't have any problems with full-colour and a lot of the books of OPP since that have very gorgeous artwork without a doubt. But still, I'm somewhat missing the good 'ol b&amp;w (and one-colour outline) arts with all their monochrominess and sketchiness. I felt that these resonated very well with the feeling of WoD and especially with vampire. I certainly don't have an exclusive preference for b&amp;w only, far from it, but I always felt that good b&amp;w has something... I don't realy know, more &quot;magic&quot; in it. One of my friends, who is more in the whole aesthetics thing than I'm sayed that is because the b&amp;w has more emphasis on strict form, contour and light and shadow and leave more to the imagination to &quot;fill the gaps&quot;. I suppose he's more or less right, but I never studied graphics or photography till now (but I want and plan to).<br /> <br /> I also thought that maybe it is an age thing, since I grown up in the late '90s and early 2000s. Maybe I'm just starting to be a grumpy old(er) who feels the styles of nowadays a little bit alien and missing the aforementioned good ol'. But, since there was and is a lot of full colour arts in various products and galeries which I liked pretty much, I'm not sure. Or It's just the case of WoD which I accostumed to being b&amp;w.<br /> <br /> So, what do you think? There was really a noticeable shift of artistic style preference in rpg products? Are you also missing the style of the '90s era?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-29T15:39:39-08:00","id":776844,"title":"","content":"I don't think they'd dislike black and white art itself. Rather, the way most older RPs look is rather...less than polished. SCAR is a joke 'round here for a reason, but they are very much an example of the way the artwork looked.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-29T16:09:44-08:00","id":776863,"title":"","content":"I agree, that, as you said less than polished arts of the past books are inferior to the majority of today's average quality. But I didn't exactly speak about quality, instead of style. B&amp;W style and full-color style, because it seems to me that (even good quality) b&amp;w is out of fashion these days and I'm curious if it's just me, or that's a trend.<br /> <br /> Another thing is the type of rpg. As I said for me, b&amp;w can be particularly good for horror games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}